<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Javad Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Javad Khan of the Ziyadoghlu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> line was the last khan of Ganja, ruling from 1786. He supported Agha Muhammad Khan Qajar against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1795. When Russian troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-tsitsianov">Pavel Tsitsianov</a> stormed Ganja in January 1804, he refused to surrender and was killed defending the fortress, and the khanate was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/javad-khan.jpg" alt="Javad Khan" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hassan.Baku · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:QS%27s_Javad_Khan_(22).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Javad was the son of Shahverdi Khan and his wife Sharafjahan Khanum. In 1778, when his elder half-brother Muhammad Khan seized the throne of Ganja and began killing his relatives, Javad fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khalil-khan">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>. He came to power in 1786, after his brother Rahim Khan was deposed through Georgian and Karabakh intervention, and was the sixth and last khan of Ganja.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His first years were dominated by pressure from King Heraclius II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, who demanded Ganja for himself. A joint Georgian and Russian march on the town in 1787 ended when the Russian battalions were recalled at the start of the Russian-Turkish war, and in early 1789 Georgian troops under Prince Vakhtang defeated Javad. The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-of-quba">Fath Ali Khan of Quba</a> in March 1789 let him recover his position. He was the first to acknowledge Agha Muhammad Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> as shah, joined him at Ganja in 1795 and guided his army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>, which the shah's forces plundered for nine days.</p><p>When the Russian expedition of 1796 under Valerian Zubov arrived, Javad handed over the keys of Ganja and admitted a Russian garrison, but the troops were withdrawn after Catherine died. In 1797 Agha Muhammad imprisoned him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a> for failing to resist the Russians; the shah was assassinated there on 17 June 1797, and Javad escaped. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-xii-of-georgia">George XII of Georgia</a> in 1800 he raided Georgian lands and claimed Kazakh and Shamshadil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-tsitsianov">Pavel Tsitsianov</a> marched on Ganja in November 1803 and claimed it as a former Georgian possession; Javad's reply cited the town's Safavid past and declared himself ready for battle. Bombardment began on 15 December, and on 14 January 1804, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> in the town opened a gate, Russian troops under Major Lisanevich took the fortress. Javad was killed by Lisanevich, and his son Hossein Qoli died hours later. Between 1,500 and 3,000 inhabitants were killed, and the fall of Ganja set off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1804-1813">Russo-Persian War</a> of 1804–1813.</p><p>Russian sources called him stubborn, and the historian Abbasgulu Bakikhanov described him as skilful and ruthless. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> he is regarded as a hero, with streets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> and Ganja named after him. His descendants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> took the surname Ziyadkhanov.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Javad Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Javad Khan</b>, who ruled from 1786, rebuilt its strength and sided with Qajar Iran against Georgia and Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganja-khanate">Ganja Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganja-azerbaijan">Ganja</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganja-azerbaijan">Ganja</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.68,46.36&amp;year=1784" aria-label="Ganja on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5686 711 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Javad Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6136 981h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6136 981h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.68,46.36&amp;year=1784">What was here in 1784? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
